Lesson 3: HTTP Caching
This is arguably the most important CDN lesson.
Why?
Because CDNs are useless without caching policies.
Many developers think:
CDN = Store File
Wrong.
The real question is:
How long should it store?
When should it refresh?
How does it know content changed?
How can stale data be avoided?
These are solved by HTTP Caching.
________________________________________
Learning Goals
After this lesson, you should understand:
✅ Cache-Control
✅ Expires
✅ ETag
✅ Last-Modified
✅ TTL
✅ 304 Not Modified
✅ Fresh vs Stale Data
✅ Browser Cache vs CDN Cache
✅ Internal Request Flow
✅ Performance Impact
✅ Failure Scenarios
✅ Interview Discussions
✅ Java Implementation
________________________________________
1. The Problem
Imagine: /logo.png
never changes.
Without caching:
User
↓
CDN
↓
Origin
↓
CDN
↓
User
every request.
________________________________________
Even worse:
10 Million Requests
All reach origin.
Huge waste.
________________________________________
Questions:
Can browser reuse old copy?
 Can CDN reuse old copy?
How long? 
How do we know file changed?
HTTP caching answers these.
________________________________________
2. HTTP Cache Hierarchy
Many engineers think only one cache exists.
Actually:
Browser Cache
↓
CDN Cache
↓
Origin Cache
↓
Database
________________________________________
Example:
User requests logo.png
Browser checks:
Do I already have it?
If yes:
Serve immediately
No network call.
________________________________________
If browser misses:
Ask CDN
If CDN has it:
Return from edge
________________________________________
If CDN misses:
Ask Origin
________________________________________
Performance Comparison
Browser Cache Hit
0 ms
Network skipped.
________________________________________
CDN Cache Hit
5–20 ms
________________________________________
Origin Hit
100–300 ms
________________________________________
Database Hit
200–1000 ms
